1.3.4 [driver-assistance system] Availability and Constraints

 On the [car manufacturer], TACC is available only when traveling above 18 mph. 9 Once TACC is
activated, Autosteer is available whenever the system detects lane markings. A driver can activate
TACC by itself but can activate Autosteer only after activating TACC; that is, Autosteer is not
available without TACC. In addition, [car manufacturer] imposes two types of constraints on the driver’s use
of [driver-assistance system]: these are (1) hard constraints that the system imposes automatically, and (2) soft
constraints that [car manufacturer] provides as cautions to the driver through the instrument screen and
instructions in the vehicle owner’s manual.

 Hard Constraints. System-imposed constraints on driver use of [driver-assistance system] include
(1) setting an upper limit on lateral acceleration that affects the system alert sequence; (2) limiting
the maximum cruising speed, depending on the detection of the speed limit on a restricted road;
and (3) measuring the level of driver engagement and deactivating [driver-assistance system] if the level is

